 the statement below, the two blanks can be filled by positive single-digit numbers in such a way that the statement is always true:\n\n$$\\text{If }2x\\equiv y+5\\ (\\bmod\\ 9)\\text{, then }x\\equiv \\underline{\\ \\ \\ }\\,y+\\underline{\\ \\ \\ }\\ (\\bmod\\ 9).$$What is the product of the two digits that go in the blanks?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a3b869e4a3c0b9d18cf33d8f55efe72f193a6edeff25867f900643cc28cfeb9a"},"seconds":11.557701826095581,"seed":300920875074190113,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given congruence:\n\\[2x \\equiv y + 5 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nWe need to find a congruence of the form:\n\\[x \\equiv \\underline{\\ \\ \\ }\\,y + \\underline{\\ \\ \\ } \\pmod{9}.\\]\nLet's denote the unknown digits in the blanks as \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). So, we want:\n\\[x \\equiv ay + b \\pmod{9}.\\]\nTo find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we need to isolate \\(x\\) in the original congruence. We do this by multiplying both sides of the congruence \\(2x \\equiv y + 5 \\pmod{9}\\) by the modular inverse of 2 modulo 9. The inverse of 2 modulo 9 is a number \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[2k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nBy testing values, we find that \\(k = 5\\) because:\n\\[2 \\cdot 5 = 10 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nSo, we multiply both sides of the original congruence by 5:\n\\[5 \\cdot 2x \\equiv 5(y + 5) \\pmod{9}.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[10x \\equiv 5y + 25 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nSince \\(10 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\\), we have:\n\\[x \\equiv 5y + 25 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nNext, we reduce 25 modulo 9:\n\\[25 \\equiv 25 - 2 \\cdot 9 = 25 - 18 = 7 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[x \\equiv 5y + 7 \\pmod{9}.\\]\nThis means that the digits in the blanks are \\(5\\) and \\(7\\). The product of these digits is:\n\\[5 \\cdot 7 = 35.\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{35}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{35}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:439"}
